Chapter: Location

 The Location effect is the right to know where the machine is. It is a
 bare marker here, the way Microphone and Network are: the operations
 that use it -- locate and altitude -- are not declared in this chapter.

 They were, once, and they were worse than unimplemented. Two effect
 operations with no handler anywhere in the tree meant a call to locate
 type-checked clean and then died at emit with CDX2040, which is a
 promise the compiler does not keep. locate's declared answer was
 `Pair Integer Integer`, and this chapter never cited Foreword chapter
 Pair -- so the name in the declaration did not resolve to the Pair that
 exists, and nothing noticed, because nothing could reach the
 declaration to find out.

 This machine has no GPS receiver and no seam where one could be read,
 so the honest arrangement is that the foreword declares the RIGHT and
 something else supplies the answer. A chapter that wants the operations cites
 Foreword chapter LocationStub, and gets fixed constants that say in their
 own names that they are fixed. A chapter that does not gets CDX3002 on
 the name -- the truth: it asked for something it did not bring.

 We say:

Section: Definitions

  effect Location where

